      <div class="about-body reveal">
        <p>
          I'm Joe Cape. I started building Moodle systems in 2010, straight out
          of an M.Sc. in Information System Development, when a Stirling social
          enterprise needed an online course and I was contracting for a local
          web agency. I picked Moodle, built their platform, and they're still
          running it fifteen years later.
        </p>
        <p>
          Most of my work since has been as a senior contractor: ten years with
          Aleido Learning in Sweden on Totara / Moodle products for regional
          government, rewarding work with several UK universities, and
          plugin and integration work for training providers and product teams.
          I've acted as sole end-to-end technical lead for organisations with no
          internal IT function, and as architectural reviewer and lead developer
          on larger teams.
        </p>
        <p>
          Working freelance for smaller organisations, I'm often involved well 
          beyond the code - in the instructional design, and the conversations about 
          what a course is actually meant to do. That closeness to the whole learning 
          experience, rather than just the build, shapes the question I keep coming 
          back to: what's it all for? How can EdTech help people learn better, and 
          leave them more capable than they were yesterday?
        </p>
